## A small set of useful ideas

Keep **building**, **publishing**, and **operating** separate. During building, change code freely in a sandbox. During publishing, produce and verify a specific version. During operation, watch the running version and keep a clear route back to a known good release.

This separation helps a person or an agent reason about what changed. A preview is evidence to inspect; a published release is a decision to serve that version.

<Callout title="A local learning reconstruction" kind="info">This project recreates documentation patterns for study. Practice progress stays in your browser. The displayed learning credits are illustrative and have no monetary value.</Callout>

## Find your way around

Use the sidebar filter to narrow the page list, or press **⌘ K** to search the documentation. The right-hand rail follows the section you are reading. Code examples share their selected language, so you can follow a tutorial in one language throughout.

The Markdown menu gives you an editable source view. Themes, navigation preferences and practice progress survive a page refresh on the same device.

